1
00:01:31,327 --> 00:01:35,624
- <font color="#D81D1D">Synced and corrected by VitoSilans</font> -
-- <font color="#138CE9">www.Addic7ed.com</font> --

2
00:01:51,654 --> 00:01:54,447
I don't have the right words
for what happened that day.

3
00:01:54,531 --> 00:01:56,998
But he was a hero, no question.

4
00:01:57,034 --> 00:01:59,458
Captain Conway's conduct was heroic.

5
00:01:59,494 --> 00:02:00,627
A hero.

6
00:02:00,663 --> 00:02:03,295
Army Captain Craig Squire had
been captured by the Taliban,

7
00:02:03,331 --> 00:02:05,547
held hostage in the Kandahar province.

8
00:02:05,583 --> 00:02:07,341
We realized the intel was bad.

9
00:02:07,377 --> 00:02:09,630
By the time we found him,
we'd started taking fire.

10
00:02:11,506 --> 00:02:13,765
It was like a death garden or something.

11
00:02:13,801 --> 00:02:16,976
Captain Conway got out of
the plane and got Squire out.

12
00:02:17,012 --> 00:02:18,231
Don't even ask me how.

13
00:02:18,931 --> 00:02:20,772
This election is too important.

14
00:02:20,808 --> 00:02:26,027
You have a choice between
Francis Underwood and a real hero.

15
00:02:26,063 --> 00:02:29,822
This is what happens when you
throw around a word like "war."

16
00:02:29,858 --> 00:02:31,443
All hell breaks loose.

17
00:02:32,695 --> 00:02:36,412
He saved Squire. He saved all of us.

18
00:02:36,448 --> 00:02:39,081
War is real.

19
00:02:39,117 --> 00:02:40,953
Paid for by Americans for Truth.

20
00:02:41,870 --> 00:02:43,745
I don't know how many times
I have to go through this,

21
00:02:43,747 --> 00:02:45,455
but my campaign had
nothing to do with that ad.

22
00:02:45,457 --> 00:02:46,798
That was a super PAC.

23
00:02:46,834 --> 00:02:49,133
- I know, but...
- But those soldiers, though.

24
00:02:49,169 --> 00:02:50,253
They're the ones.

25
00:02:50,838 --> 00:02:52,887
This country owes them a lot.

26
00:02:52,923 --> 00:02:54,672
- But that ad is on all the time.
- You wanna know why?

27
00:02:54,674 --> 00:02:57,934
Because Frank Underwood has
failed to keep this country safe.

28
00:02:57,970 --> 00:02:59,143
The death of James Miller,

29
00:02:59,179 --> 00:03:03,022
the so-called ICO attack at
Capital East Telecom network...

30
00:03:03,058 --> 00:03:05,441
Wait, wait, wait. Are you
saying ICO wasn't responsible?

31
00:03:05,477 --> 00:03:06,693
I'm saying it doesn't matter.

32
00:03:06,729 --> 00:03:09,486
The Internet went down
on the president's watch.

33
00:03:09,522 --> 00:03:11,698
- Don't you think ICO was...
- Now listen, Fred...

34
00:03:11,734 --> 00:03:14,826
- No, ICO is a real threat.
- There's a lot of people waiting

35
00:03:14,862 --> 00:03:17,244
and I promised one question per person.

36
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:21,118
It was great talking to you, and
please don't forget to vote tomorrow.

37
00:03:22,702 --> 00:03:24,537
Now, how long have
we been going, anyway?

38
00:03:25,789 --> 00:03:27,588
- Four hours.
- Four hours.

39
00:03:27,624 --> 00:03:29,298
That means we got 20 hours to go

40
00:03:29,334 --> 00:03:31,926
and I haven't even had the chance
to thank my friends at Pollyhop

41
00:03:31,962 --> 00:03:35,221
for making this all possible
and live streaming this event.

42
00:03:35,257 --> 00:03:36,638
Benjamin Grant and his crew.

43
00:03:36,674 --> 00:03:39,641
All right. What's the next question?

44
00:03:39,677 --> 00:03:43,020
Annie? Annie from Maine.
What's on your mind?

45
00:03:43,056 --> 00:03:44,396
Was Fred one of ours?

46
00:03:44,432 --> 00:03:46,357
I think so. We're gonna
keep hitting the ad.

47
00:03:46,393 --> 00:03:50,486
LeAnn's got callers lined
up. Are you there yet?

48
00:03:50,522 --> 00:03:53,155
Almost. Then back on the plane.

49
00:03:53,191 --> 00:03:55,366
- I'm about to go on.
- Ohio, still?

50
00:03:55,402 --> 00:03:58,410
Oh, yeah. And how's he looking? Conway?

51
00:03:58,446 --> 00:04:00,747
Well, he's only four hours
in. It's not sustainable.

52
00:04:00,783 --> 00:04:03,415
- But is he commanding?
- He's arrogant, Francis.

53
00:04:03,451 --> 00:04:05,584
- Mr. President?
- I have to go.

54
00:04:05,620 --> 00:04:07,539
Okay. See you in Gettysburg.

55
00:04:08,581 --> 00:04:11,716
You're saying it doesn't matter if
Underwood knew about pay-for-play?

56
00:04:11,752 --> 00:04:15,052
Well, I'm saying it's bad either way.

57
00:04:15,088 --> 00:04:18,680
He either stole the
presidency from his own party,

58
00:04:18,716 --> 00:04:22,309
or he was vice president
in a corrupt administration.

59
00:04:22,345 --> 00:04:25,432
But it's important that we
let the committee do its job.

60
00:04:26,016 --> 00:04:27,648
So thanks for your call, Annie.

61
00:04:27,684 --> 00:04:30,818
Arthur Capo from Del Rey Beach,
Hawaii. Arthur, how you doing?

62
00:04:30,854 --> 00:04:32,981
Why did they want to talk to you?

63
00:04:33,899 --> 00:04:36,407
I'm sorry, who? Uh, what's
your question, Arthur?

64
00:04:36,443 --> 00:04:38,403
Josh Masterson and Zachary...

65
00:04:39,571 --> 00:04:42,914
I forget his last name. But the
terrorists who killed Jim Miller.

66
00:04:42,950 --> 00:04:45,291
Right. Uh, well...

67
00:04:45,327 --> 00:04:47,035
All I know is what they said

68
00:04:47,037 --> 00:04:48,627
in their list of demands.

69
00:04:48,663 --> 00:04:51,380
And they believed I would
be elected president.

70
00:04:51,416 --> 00:04:54,383
But really, why? Why you?

71
00:04:54,419 --> 00:04:58,429
I successfully negotiated the release
of Caroline and Melissa Miller.

72
00:04:58,465 --> 00:05:01,426
- But not Jim Miller.
- I wasn't given the chance.

73
00:05:02,970 --> 00:05:06,306
What's your read on him, the
way he answers those questions?

74
00:05:07,765 --> 00:05:10,393
- He's hiding something.
- Right.

75
00:05:11,436 --> 00:05:12,813
But it doesn't matter.

76
00:05:13,897 --> 00:05:17,442
He's modern. He makes
the presidency accessible.

77
00:05:18,610 --> 00:05:21,738
I mean, don't you want to see
the candidate stay up all night?

78
00:05:27,660 --> 00:05:29,662
Are you calling Francis old?

79
00:05:40,757 --> 00:05:43,682
I appreciate the applause,
ladies and gentlemen,

80
00:05:43,718 --> 00:05:48,145
but I imagine it's for the
woman you really came to see.

81
00:05:50,808 --> 00:05:53,896
"These are dangerous times."

82
00:05:54,812 --> 00:05:56,403
These are dangerous times.

83
00:05:56,439 --> 00:05:58,197
...of the United States,

84
00:05:58,233 --> 00:06:01,111
Claire Underwood.

85
00:06:02,195 --> 00:06:04,990
He's only a couple years
older than me, you know.

86
00:06:17,169 --> 00:06:20,511
Thank you. Thank you, Nevada!

87
00:06:26,094 --> 00:06:29,514
I really appreciate you all
coming out here this morning.

88
00:06:31,599 --> 00:06:36,944
No substitute for retail
politics. Especially her brand.

89
00:06:36,980 --> 00:06:38,523
Thank you.

90
00:06:39,942 --> 00:06:41,740
Could you get me some water, please?

91
00:06:41,776 --> 00:06:43,653
Hello, everyone.

92
00:06:44,779 --> 00:06:46,203
Thank you so much.

93
00:06:46,239 --> 00:06:48,200
Thank you very much. That's very kind.

94
00:06:49,742 --> 00:06:51,625
Oh, thanks so much.

95
00:06:51,661 --> 00:06:54,545
Oh, now stop that. Stop it, stop
it. Enough, enough, enough. Enough.

96
00:06:54,581 --> 00:06:57,131
It's embarrassing,
all of that adulation.

97
00:06:57,167 --> 00:07:00,009
Hello, nice to see you. Thank
you so much for coming out.

98
00:07:00,045 --> 00:07:01,379
Much appreciated.

99
00:07:02,422 --> 00:07:04,680
- Jack!
- Great speech, Mr. President.

100
00:07:04,716 --> 00:07:06,091
It's like you're saying
what I'm thinking.

101
00:07:06,093 --> 00:07:09,560
Well, that is the idea. Thank you.

102
00:07:09,596 --> 00:07:11,979
Uh, I'm a little worried
about the Conway thing, though.

103
00:07:12,015 --> 00:07:15,149
- Bringing up a lot of questions.
- But not a lot of answers.

104
00:07:15,185 --> 00:07:17,435
Yeah, and we don't need to
keep hearing about that article

105
00:07:17,437 --> 00:07:19,395
the day before the election.
What are we doing about it?

106
00:07:19,397 --> 00:07:21,530
Well, we're here, Jack.

107
00:07:21,566 --> 00:07:24,866
We are fighting back
one handshake at a time.

108
00:07:24,902 --> 00:07:28,787
Gwendolyn, you look absolutely stunning.
May I get a photograph, please, Steve?

109
00:07:28,823 --> 00:07:31,576
Could you do this, please?
Make sure you cut him out.

110
00:07:32,535 --> 00:07:36,128
We have Jeff from Corpus Christi,
Texas. How are you doing today, Jeff?

111
00:07:36,164 --> 00:07:38,547
We saw the ad. About the rescue.

112
00:07:38,583 --> 00:07:42,009
You know, Jeff, I feel like we
have been through this already.

113
00:07:42,045 --> 00:07:43,630
You saved my brother's life, sir.

114
00:07:44,589 --> 00:07:45,923
Craig Squire.

115
00:07:47,384 --> 00:07:50,887
Captain Squire. That's...
that's your brother?

116
00:07:54,141 --> 00:07:55,558
Captain.

117
00:07:59,187 --> 00:08:00,522
Remember me?

118
00:08:01,564 --> 00:08:03,822
I do remember. Of course I remember.

119
00:08:03,858 --> 00:08:05,992
I didn't think you wanted talk about it.

120
00:08:06,028 --> 00:08:09,161
We lived through it.
That's enough, isn't it?

121
00:08:09,197 --> 00:08:12,039
I never got to thank you and tell you...

122
00:08:12,075 --> 00:08:15,209
Captain Squire, you
would've done the same.

123
00:08:15,245 --> 00:08:18,670
I don't know. I was in there.

124
00:08:18,706 --> 00:08:21,798
They were tossing bodies on top
of me, one after the other...

125
00:08:21,834 --> 00:08:23,003
I was already dead.

126
00:08:24,004 --> 00:08:27,888
That's not right. It's like
I've been dead once already.

127
00:08:27,924 --> 00:08:30,682
But the bodies... the way
they were stuck together.

128
00:08:30,718 --> 00:08:32,476
And the heat and the smell...

129
00:08:32,512 --> 00:08:34,561
But you know when you were
pulling me out, remember?

130
00:08:34,597 --> 00:08:39,816
I... I don't remember much, actually.
It all happened pretty quick.

131
00:08:39,852 --> 00:08:40,985
I know you heard it.

132
00:08:41,021 --> 00:08:42,987
You have to get him
out of this question.

133
00:08:43,023 --> 00:08:44,855
He doesn't like talking
about this. You know that.

134
00:08:44,857 --> 00:08:47,324
- Hannah, let's talk.
- Come on, man. I know you did.

135
00:08:47,360 --> 00:08:50,536
I was just trying to get us
out of there as fast as I could.

136
00:08:50,572 --> 00:08:53,075
Craig, we're here.

137
00:08:54,576 --> 00:08:56,833
- We made it.
- Yeah, but...

138
00:08:56,869 --> 00:08:59,961
- You gotta live your life.
- Thanks for taking our call.

139
00:08:59,997 --> 00:09:01,374
Thank you.

140
00:09:07,880 --> 00:09:10,423
He didn't call him out enough.
He didn't get Conway to...

141
00:09:10,425 --> 00:09:13,095
Look... he did what he could.

142
00:09:14,887 --> 00:09:17,271
What did he hear? What
did he mean by that?

143
00:09:17,307 --> 00:09:18,939
I don't know. It's between Will and him.

144
00:09:18,975 --> 00:09:22,401
He's wanted Will to come clean
about it for a long time, but...

145
00:09:22,437 --> 00:09:23,646
it didn't happen.

146
00:09:24,564 --> 00:09:27,739
Which is why, for Craig's sake, we
need to get them back on the phone.

147
00:09:27,775 --> 00:09:31,743
Miss Harvey, you've
been on us for months.

148
00:09:31,779 --> 00:09:35,164
I'm sorry it didn't work out. If
this means the deal's off, okay.

149
00:09:35,200 --> 00:09:36,790
We can always find a place to go.

150
00:09:36,826 --> 00:09:39,085
Just let me talk to him again.
We wouldn't want him to...

151
00:09:39,121 --> 00:09:41,295
Jesus Christ! My brother
has been through enough.

152
00:09:41,331 --> 00:09:42,582
We're done.

153
00:09:43,916 --> 00:09:45,252
I need you to leave.

154
00:09:58,431 --> 00:09:59,474
He's doing great.

155
00:10:00,267 --> 00:10:01,601
Is he?

156
00:10:02,560 --> 00:10:03,728
That ad's playing for us.

157
00:10:07,064 --> 00:10:10,116
Look, I... I agreed to this.

158
00:10:10,152 --> 00:10:12,618
- But I know my husband.
- I know you do.

159
00:10:12,654 --> 00:10:15,662
And those questions
were to be expected, yes?

160
00:10:15,698 --> 00:10:17,909
It's a good thing
Squire changed his mind.

161
00:10:19,202 --> 00:10:22,211
We can pull some tape and put
something together tonight.

162
00:10:22,247 --> 00:10:24,166
Hit some markets in the morning.

163
00:10:24,874 --> 00:10:26,293
When it's time to vote...

164
00:10:27,335 --> 00:10:28,753
Will saved his life.

165
00:10:30,213 --> 00:10:31,506
This is too much.

166
00:10:32,424 --> 00:10:34,225
I don't want any more
questions about the war.

167
00:10:34,259 --> 00:10:36,142
That's not gonna work.

168
00:10:36,178 --> 00:10:37,684
Look, I can show you numbers,

169
00:10:37,720 --> 00:10:39,895
I can show you how well
it's all playing for us.

170
00:10:39,931 --> 00:10:44,065
- What I'm saying, Mark, is that this...
- I've been at this a long time, Hannah.

171
00:10:44,101 --> 00:10:46,318
You know, when Marshall
told me you were available...

172
00:10:46,354 --> 00:10:50,107
Marshall and I go way back. The
timing is right for all of us, right?

173
00:10:51,901 --> 00:10:54,451
- We're very grateful.
- He will tell you, I don't get involved,

174
00:10:54,487 --> 00:10:58,241
not this late in the game, unless
I know I can make a difference.

175
00:11:01,118 --> 00:11:03,502
So whatever happened to
Will with Squire, whatever...

176
00:11:03,538 --> 00:11:06,416
- It's not...
- I don't wanna know. I don't need to know.

177
00:11:07,834 --> 00:11:10,628
But whatever pain he's
carrying about what happened...

178
00:11:12,297 --> 00:11:13,506
it's working for us.

179
00:11:14,632 --> 00:11:16,848
So, this is what I need from you.

180
00:11:16,884 --> 00:11:21,806
To go back out there and just make
sure he makes it through until morning.

181
00:11:25,852 --> 00:11:28,313
Hey.

182
00:11:30,773 --> 00:11:32,400
He has a chance to be a fine president.

183
00:11:33,526 --> 00:11:35,862
A great president.

184
00:11:38,323 --> 00:11:41,243
Even better.

185
00:11:42,244 --> 00:11:43,745
Because we could use one.

186
00:11:47,249 --> 00:11:51,049
There's a reason that Squire
didn't appear in that ad.

187
00:11:51,085 --> 00:11:52,962
I'm just nailing down the specifics.

188
00:11:56,883 --> 00:11:59,933
- Let's bring Doug in on this.
- He's on his way to Philly.

189
00:11:59,969 --> 00:12:01,935
And with all due respect, sir,

190
00:12:01,971 --> 00:12:04,140
his skill set doesn't
necessarily apply here.

191
00:12:05,892 --> 00:12:08,275
Do you know how long Doug
has worked for me, LeAnn?

192
00:12:08,311 --> 00:12:09,360
Not exactly, no.

193
00:12:09,396 --> 00:12:12,529
Well, it's just this side of
forever. I know everything about him.

194
00:12:12,565 --> 00:12:15,366
His strengths, his
weaknesses, his skill set

195
00:12:15,402 --> 00:12:18,112
and the contents of his soul.

196
00:12:19,489 --> 00:12:23,410
I hope you're not
questioning my loyalty.

197
00:12:25,161 --> 00:12:27,878
I just like to know about the
people who work for me, LeAnn.

198
00:12:27,914 --> 00:12:31,840
- I'm pretty straightforward.
- I don't believe that for a second.

199
00:12:31,876 --> 00:12:33,842
The first time I sat down with Doug,

200
00:12:33,878 --> 00:12:36,553
it was more of a confession
than a conversation.

201
00:12:36,589 --> 00:12:40,927
He told me about his drinking, his
past. He was completely open with me.

202
00:12:42,219 --> 00:12:44,520
I don't think we can
rely on Craig Squire.

203
00:12:44,556 --> 00:12:46,516
Then why am I still talking to you?

204
00:12:47,517 --> 00:12:50,562
I'm not hiding the
contents of my soul, sir.

205
00:12:51,187 --> 00:12:54,905
- Reach out to Vanessa Morrison.
- I already reached out to the director.

206
00:12:54,941 --> 00:12:59,034
Yes, but Doug hasn't. So have him do it
and then you come up with something else

207
00:12:59,070 --> 00:13:01,912
before Conway is done
with his little sideshow.

208
00:13:01,948 --> 00:13:03,830
And in terms of what comes next,

209
00:13:03,866 --> 00:13:06,875
I adjusted the opening remarks
and cut some from the stump.

210
00:13:06,911 --> 00:13:08,335
The stump is just fine.

211
00:13:08,371 --> 00:13:09,851
Thank you very much.

212
00:13:11,958 --> 00:13:13,549
Get me the First Lady, please.

213
00:13:13,585 --> 00:13:15,926
Well, the... the short answer is

214
00:13:15,962 --> 00:13:17,928
in a democracy, everyone
has the same rights...

215
00:13:17,964 --> 00:13:20,049
The same opportunities.

216
00:13:21,468 --> 00:13:22,516
Hello.

217
00:13:22,552 --> 00:13:25,096
Do the people around us
understand what it takes?

218
00:13:26,431 --> 00:13:28,647
Look, I know Conway's getting a bump,

219
00:13:28,683 --> 00:13:31,608
but it doesn't mean we can't
surmount it in the next 24 hours.

220
00:13:31,644 --> 00:13:35,357
Do they know what we
have done to get this far?

221
00:13:36,691 --> 00:13:38,574
Tom is sitting across from me right now.

222
00:13:38,610 --> 00:13:40,945
I don't care who you're
sitting next to, Claire.

223
00:13:42,530 --> 00:13:46,117
We're all doing everything
we can as fast as we can.

224
00:13:49,537 --> 00:13:51,837
I don't like it. I
don't like chasing this.

225
00:13:51,873 --> 00:13:54,667
What's your next stop?

226
00:13:55,585 --> 00:14:00,507
- We, uh... We're in Scranton in 20.
- You need to save your voice.

227
00:14:02,800 --> 00:14:05,302
I'll see you on the battlefield.

228
00:14:14,771 --> 00:14:15,980
How's the president doing?

229
00:14:18,775 --> 00:14:21,444
He's acting like he's
still 30 years old.

230
00:14:26,115 --> 00:14:27,116
We both are.

231
00:14:29,118 --> 00:14:32,414
Is that good... or bad?

232
00:14:34,916 --> 00:14:37,126
That depends on if we win or lose.

233
00:14:38,795 --> 00:14:43,138
Because I'm a vet and I'm still
not clear on exactly what happened.

234
00:14:43,174 --> 00:14:46,558
You know, I think everything has been
said by the soldiers who were there.

235
00:14:46,594 --> 00:14:51,271
No. 'Cause your plane, was it shot
down or was it a planned rescue?

236
00:14:51,307 --> 00:14:53,476
Planned, yes.

237
00:14:54,101 --> 00:14:56,527
Until the plan had to change.

238
00:14:56,563 --> 00:15:00,071
- I think you get the idea.
- No, I don't get it, sir.

239
00:15:00,107 --> 00:15:01,990
- You were a pilot, right?
- Yes.

240
00:15:02,026 --> 00:15:04,325
So why were your boots
even on the ground?

241
