I know who I'm voting for.

487
00:35:23,288 --> 00:35:26,839
I'd like to thank everyone who
made the last 24 hours possible.

488
00:35:26,875 --> 00:35:28,966
All the... the technicians and crew.

489
00:35:29,002 --> 00:35:33,512
And if we all work together the
way they have, then I promise,

490
00:35:33,548 --> 00:35:37,725
we will truly make this the
first of many, many new days

491
00:35:37,761 --> 00:35:40,013
for the greatest country in the world.

492
00:35:41,890 --> 00:35:45,936
Good morning, America. God
bless America, and thank you.

493
00:35:47,688 --> 00:35:51,524
And we're out. Okay, guys.

494
00:35:59,908 --> 00:36:01,242
You did great.

495
00:36:01,993 --> 00:36:03,870
- You did it.
- Yeah.

496
00:36:05,121 --> 00:36:06,915
Everyone is so pleased.

497
00:36:13,379 --> 00:36:16,675
I know it was tough,
but you got through it.

498
00:36:21,137 --> 00:36:23,306
- I'm really proud of you.
- Thank you.

499
00:36:29,437 --> 00:36:32,154
Just some questions
that I needed to answer.

500
00:36:32,190 --> 00:36:36,194
I know. But you did it. You did it.

501
00:36:44,995 --> 00:36:46,961
- Great job. Great.
- That was great.

502
00:36:46,997 --> 00:36:48,373
- Amazing.
- Thank you.

503
00:36:55,296 --> 00:36:58,305
Testing, testing. One, two, three.

504
00:36:58,341 --> 00:37:01,851
Testing. One, two, three.

505
00:37:01,887 --> 00:37:05,681
Uh, a little more gain.
A skosh more reverb.

506
00:37:06,599 --> 00:37:12,355
Uh, testing, testing, testing.
That's good. That's good. Mmm.

507
00:37:13,815 --> 00:37:18,200
I would like to thank the American people
for their overwhelming support tonight.

508
00:37:18,236 --> 00:37:21,286
As your next president, I do
not take this mandate lightly.

509
00:37:21,322 --> 00:37:25,124
In fact... it is heavy as hell.

510
00:37:25,160 --> 00:37:27,495
You got a better chance
than Underwood does.

511
00:37:34,169 --> 00:37:35,879
Those are very high heels, Bridget.

512
00:37:37,839 --> 00:37:40,216
When Underwood loses,
the committee goes away.

513
00:37:41,051 --> 00:37:42,718
You're breaking my heart.

514
00:37:43,594 --> 00:37:47,312
I was looking forward to hearing what
you had to say about Doug Stamper.

515
00:37:47,348 --> 00:37:48,989
Yeah, I was looking
forward to saying it.

516
00:37:50,936 --> 00:37:52,603
I came here to thank you.

517
00:37:53,646 --> 00:37:57,025
I took it down... off the Internet.

518
00:37:59,360 --> 00:38:02,280
It's weird, talking to
you about this. I mean...

519
00:38:03,198 --> 00:38:05,366
uh, you saw it.

520
00:38:07,828 --> 00:38:11,503
- My brother could've seen that.
- Do I remind you of your brother?

521
00:38:13,708 --> 00:38:17,843
- He's a little more present.
- Well, it's my job to look ahead.

522
00:38:17,879 --> 00:38:20,799
Hmm. Good luck with that.

523
00:38:40,485 --> 00:38:42,952
- Thank you so very much.
- Thank you, sir.

524
00:38:42,988 --> 00:38:43,989
Oh.

525
00:38:53,957 --> 00:38:56,548
- We have the overnight numbers.
- Give me the outliers.

526
00:38:56,584 --> 00:39:00,510
Minus two in New York. Plus two
in New Hampshire. Both not bad.

527
00:39:00,546 --> 00:39:04,098
"Half-empty, half-full" is an
expression I have never cared for.

528
00:39:04,134 --> 00:39:05,933
The call you made last
night, it's helping.

529
00:39:05,969 --> 00:39:07,343
We grabbed the headlines this morning.

530
00:39:07,345 --> 00:39:08,685
- Doug?
- Yes, sir.

531
00:39:08,721 --> 00:39:12,314
I want you to reach out to Sally Ann back
in Gaffney and get me the turnout so far.

532
00:39:12,350 --> 00:39:14,231
I can get you the numbers for the state.

533
00:39:14,685 --> 00:39:17,903
No, I don't want that. I
want the Gaffney turnout.

534
00:39:17,939 --> 00:39:20,405
State numbers come in, you
give them to us as they come.

535
00:39:20,441 --> 00:39:22,282
Anything out of the
ordinary, you let us know.

536
00:39:22,318 --> 00:39:25,035
Other than that, we're
done. Oh, and, Doug?

537
00:39:25,071 --> 00:39:28,366
- Yes, sir.
- You'll know where to find us.

538
00:39:33,579 --> 00:39:35,706
You're worried about
the crowd we just saw.

539
00:39:36,832 --> 00:39:40,628
Not the size. Their enthusiasm.

540
00:39:48,678 --> 00:39:50,221
I don't think so.

541
00:39:52,057 --> 00:39:54,142
Jerry, I'm gonna have to call you back.

542
00:39:55,226 --> 00:39:57,692
This is 9:00 a.m. turnout in
our base precincts or swing?

543
00:39:57,728 --> 00:39:59,236
Base.

544
00:39:59,272 --> 00:40:00,606
Shit.

545
00:40:03,526 --> 00:40:05,068
I've got different numbers.
You're gonna need...

546
00:40:05,070 --> 00:40:07,072
I did confirm. Twice.

547
00:40:11,952 --> 00:40:14,412
Doug, it's me. Call me
as soon as you get this.

548
00:40:15,914 --> 00:40:19,506
You handle just automobile
insurance or all kinds?

549
00:40:19,542 --> 00:40:22,509
All kinds. Fire, earthquake,
theft, public liability.

550
00:40:22,545 --> 00:40:25,637
Group insurance, industrial stuff,
and so on right down the line.

551
00:40:25,673 --> 00:40:26,889
Accident insurance?

552
00:40:26,925 --> 00:40:29,385
Accident insurance?
Sure, Mrs. Dietrichson.

553
00:40:31,221 --> 00:40:32,845
Wish you'd tell me what's
engraved on that anklet.

554
00:40:32,847 --> 00:40:35,183
- Just my name.
- As for instance?

555
00:40:35,891 --> 00:40:39,520
- Phyllis.
- Phyllis, huh? I think I like that.

556
00:40:40,105 --> 00:40:41,486
Let's do this one.

557
00:40:41,522 --> 00:40:44,323
I'd drive it around the
block a couple of times.

558
00:40:44,359 --> 00:40:47,867
Mr. Neff, why don't you drop by tomorrow
evening around 8:30? He'll be in then.

559
00:40:47,903 --> 00:40:50,329
- Who?
- My husband.

560
00:40:50,365 --> 00:40:52,115
You were anxious to
talk to him, weren't you?

561
00:40:52,117 --> 00:40:54,208
Yeah, I was. But, uh...

562
00:40:54,244 --> 00:40:56,564
I'm sort of getting over the
idea, if you know what I mean.

563
00:40:56,579 --> 00:40:58,753
There's a speed limit
in this state, Mr. Neff.

564
00:40:58,789 --> 00:41:00,214
Forty-five miles an hour.

565
00:41:00,250 --> 00:41:03,592
- How fast was I going, Officer?
- I'd say around 90.

566
00:41:03,628 --> 00:41:06,011
Suppose you get off that
motorcycle and give me a ticket.

567
00:41:06,047 --> 00:41:08,138
So I'd let you off with
a warning this time.

568
00:41:08,174 --> 00:41:09,932
Suppose it doesn't take.

569
00:41:09,968 --> 00:41:11,768
Suppose I have to whack
you over the knuckles.

570
00:41:12,387 --> 00:41:14,747
Suppose I burst out crying and
put my head on your shoulder.

571
00:41:15,431 --> 00:41:17,591
Suppose you try putting that
on my husband's shoulders.

572
00:41:18,643 --> 00:41:19,810
That tears it.

573
00:41:23,481 --> 00:41:24,947
I'm just not in the mood...

574
00:41:24,983 --> 00:41:27,233
Oh, no. Come on, it's tradition.
If Doug needs us, he'll call.

575
00:41:27,235 --> 00:41:30,202
Yes, but it's different this
time. There's so much going on...

576
00:41:30,238 --> 00:41:31,779
- I know, but call me superstitious.
- We're in here.

577
00:41:31,781 --> 00:41:34,789
But we have done this every
election we have ever won.

578
00:41:34,825 --> 00:41:37,334
- Come on.
- I wonder if I know what you mean.

579
00:41:37,370 --> 00:41:39,289
I wonder if you wonder.

580
00:41:43,043 --> 00:41:44,174
It was a hot afternoon,

581
00:41:44,210 --> 00:41:47,547
and I still remember the smell of
honeysuckle all down that street.

582
00:41:48,312 --> 00:41:49,963
How could I have known that murder...

583
00:41:49,965 --> 00:41:52,766
Can sometimes smell like honeysuckle?

584
00:41:52,802 --> 00:41:54,135
Maybe you would've known, Keyes,

585
00:41:54,137 --> 00:41:55,761
the minute she mentioned
accident insurance.

586
00:41:55,763 --> 00:41:59,231
But I didn't. I felt like a million.

587
00:41:59,267 --> 00:42:00,482
Yes.

588
00:42:00,518 --> 00:42:03,438
I went back to the office
to see if I had any mail.

589
00:42:04,106 --> 00:42:06,357
Well, what do you mean they don't match?

590
00:42:07,400 --> 00:42:09,194
In my home state?

591
00:42:12,363 --> 00:42:15,622
Well, you and LeAnn better
get over here right now.

592
00:42:15,658 --> 00:42:17,243
What's the matter?

593
00:42:18,453 --> 00:42:20,494
There's something wrong with
the numbers in the precincts

594
00:42:20,496 --> 00:42:22,921
that we're getting from this
morning. They don't match up.

595
00:42:22,957 --> 00:42:25,543
It's never happened
before in South Carolina.

596
00:42:28,921 --> 00:42:32,764
- Well, you have nothing to say about that?
- Oh, I'm sure the numbers are wrong.

597
00:42:32,800 --> 00:42:34,802
Oh, you don't believe that for a second.

598
00:42:36,054 --> 00:42:38,723
I can't win with you.

599
00:42:40,933 --> 00:42:42,477
No matter what I say.

600
00:42:43,686 --> 00:42:46,653
How many times did you walk me home?

601
00:42:46,689 --> 00:42:48,066
What?

602
00:42:50,610 --> 00:42:53,785
Oh, I don't know. Probably a handful.

603
00:42:53,821 --> 00:42:58,868
No, it wasn't a handful.
It was 18... in a month.

604
00:43:03,748 --> 00:43:06,334
It was on my way home.

605
00:43:07,585 --> 00:43:10,219
- And what are you saying?
- Nothing. It was on my way home.

606
00:43:10,255 --> 00:43:11,970
No, it wasn't just on your way home.

607
00:43:12,006 --> 00:43:15,640
We tried to go to the Museum of Fine Arts.
It was closed. It was freezing that day.

608
00:43:15,676 --> 00:43:17,601
- Yeah.
- And this was on in your room.

609
00:43:17,637 --> 00:43:20,723
- It was.
- And I asked you to kiss me.

610
00:43:21,349 --> 00:43:23,773
- I made you kiss me.
- No, you didn't make me kiss you.

611
00:43:23,809 --> 00:43:25,395
I wanted to kiss you.

612
00:43:27,397 --> 00:43:31,734
And anyway, I knew we were gonna
spend the rest of our lives together.

613
00:43:32,527 --> 00:43:34,326
And we are.

614
00:43:34,362 --> 00:43:36,239
No regrets, no matter what.

615
00:43:38,073 --> 00:43:41,083
What does that mean? What do you
mean by that, "no matter what"?

616
00:43:41,119 --> 00:43:43,121
- You think we're gonna lose?
- No!

617
00:43:45,415 --> 00:43:46,416
No.

618
00:43:54,132 --> 00:43:58,475
- Are we together?
- Yes, we're together. No matter what...

619
00:43:58,511 --> 00:44:01,472
No, not "no matter
what." Are we together?

620
00:44:03,183 --> 00:44:05,274
We're together, Francis.

621
00:44:05,310 --> 00:44:08,318
Then don't ever bring up
losing around me again.

622
00:44:08,354 --> 00:44:11,774
- That's the boy.
- Thank you, Mr. Keyes.

623
00:44:15,528 --> 00:44:17,369
What kind of an outfit is this, anyway?

624
00:44:17,405 --> 00:44:19,988
Are we an insurance company or
just a bunch of dim-witted amateurs

625
00:44:19,990 --> 00:44:21,282
to write a policy on a mug like that?

626
00:44:21,284 --> 00:44:22,908
Now, wait a minute, Keyes.
I don't rate this beef.

627
00:44:22,910 --> 00:44:25,294
The turnout in Philly is
lower than they're saying.

628
00:44:25,330 --> 00:44:28,004
- How much lower?
- 30% in our key precincts.

629
00:44:28,040 --> 00:44:29,923
Thirty? How?

630
00:44:29,959 --> 00:44:31,792
It's not just Pennsylvania.
People aren't voting.

631
00:44:31,794 --> 00:44:32,967
Their people, our people.

632
00:44:33,003 --> 00:44:35,804
Wait. If Conway's got
Pennsylvania, then he's got Ohio.

633
00:44:35,840 --> 00:44:37,889
If he's got Ohio, then we lose.

634
00:44:37,925 --> 00:44:39,641
- How did this happen?
- I'll tell them.

635
00:44:39,677 --> 00:44:41,596
That is one thing you are not doing.

636
00:44:45,015 --> 00:44:46,536
That was all there was to it.

637
00:44:46,559 --> 00:44:49,276
Nothing had slipped,
nothing had been overlooked.

638
00:44:49,312 --> 00:44:51,695
There was nothing to give us away.

639
00:44:51,731 --> 00:44:55,324
And yet, Keyes, as I was walking
down the street to the drugstore,

640
00:44:55,360 --> 00:44:58,910
suddenly it came over me that
everything would go wrong.

641
00:44:58,946 --> 00:45:02,038
It sounds crazy, Keyes,
but it's true, so help me.

642
00:45:02,074 --> 00:45:04,285
I couldn't hear my own footsteps.

643
00:45:07,997 --> 00:45:09,915
It was the walk of a dead man.

644
00:45:13,669 --> 00:45:16,094
That was the longest night
I ever lived through, Keyes.

645
00:45:16,130 --> 00:45:18,841
And the next day was worse when
the story broke in the papers.

646
00:45:20,009 --> 00:45:22,058
They started talking
about it at the office.

647
00:45:22,094 --> 00:45:25,229
And the day after that, when
you started digging into it.

648
00:45:25,265 --> 00:45:29,316
I kept my hands in my pockets
because I thought they were shaking.

649
00:45:29,352 --> 00:45:31,735
I put on dark glasses so
people couldn't see my eyes,

650
00:45:31,771 --> 00:45:35,572
then I took them off again so they
wouldn't get to wondering why I wore them.

651
00:45:35,608 --> 00:45:38,700
- I tried to hold myself together but...
- Thank you.

652
00:45:38,736 --> 00:45:41,572
I could feel my nerves
pulling me to pieces.

653
00:45:48,078 --> 00:45:51,505
Get me Sally Ann in the Cherokee
County clerk's office in South Carolina.

654
00:45:51,541 --> 00:45:52,875
I'll hold.

655
00:45:55,795 --> 00:45:59,632
They overestimated the
turnouts in Pennsylvania by 30%.

656
00:46:03,177 --> 00:46:05,394
Hi! Hi, y'all.

657
00:46:05,430 --> 00:46:07,437
Well, now, do not call me "sir."

658
00:46:07,473 --> 00:46:11,065
This is Francis Underwood. We've
known each other too long for that.

659
00:46:13,062 --> 00:46:15,820
So, Sally, how was turnout today?

660
00:46:15,856 --> 00:46:18,943
Well, I was just really actually
looking for Cherokee County.

661
00:46:21,487 --> 00:46:23,781
Yes, I just wanted
to hear it for myself.

662
00:46:24,740 --> 00:46:27,749
Great. Thank you.

663
00:46:27,785 --> 00:46:30,835
And we send our love
to Henry and the boys.

664
00:46:30,871 --> 00:46:32,373
Goodbye.

665
00:46:36,377 --> 00:46:39,547
- It's low.
- What do you mean, "low"?

666
00:46:45,094 --> 00:46:46,471
Low.

667
00:49:03,691 --> 00:49:07,617
- I was thinking about that anklet.
- And what are you thinking about now?

668
00:49:07,653 --> 00:49:08,946
I'm all through thinking, baby.

669
00:49:10,280 --> 00:49:14,415
- I just came to say goodbye.
- Goodbye? Where are you going?

670
00:49:14,451 --> 00:49:16,960
You're the one that's
going, baby. Not me.

671
00:49:16,996 --> 00:49:19,588
I'm getting off the trolley
car right at this corner.

672
00:49:19,624 --> 00:49:22,249
Suppose you stop being fancy.
Let's have it, whatever it is.

673
00:49:22,251 --> 00:49:26,385
All right, I'll tell you. Friend
of mine's got a funny theory...

674
00:49:26,421 --> 00:49:28,423
You know what this means, don't you?

675
00:49:29,341 --> 00:49:31,218
This is our house.

676
00:49:32,427 --> 00:49:34,471
We are not leaving.

677
00:49:34,964 --> 00:49:36,555
The last stop's a cemetery.

678
00:49:38,979 --> 00:49:45,232
- <font color="#D81D1D">Synced and corrected by VitoSilans</font> - 
